<description>
Extend each path object to its matched marker and recolor an equal-length tail from the path's opposite endpoint.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Non-background pixels form the same number of isolated marker pixels and 4-connected monochrome path objects.
- Each path object is a one-pixel-wide, unbranched orthogonal path with exactly two endpoints.
- Every marker has a clear row or column sightline to at least one path endpoint, and the global marker-to-path assignment is unique.

Rule
- Match markers to path objects by the unique one-to-one assignment where each marker sees its object's endpoint along a straight background gap.
- Fill that gap with the matched path object's color, leaving the marker pixel itself unchanged.
- From the opposite endpoint of the same path, recolor as many existing path cells with the marker color as there were gap cells.

Key insight
- The marker does not simply color the nearby endpoint; its distance from the path measures how much of the far end of that path is converted.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "isolated marker pixel s",
  "dst": "one-pixel-wide unbranched path object d",
  "match_cond": "s has a clear horizontal or vertical bg sightline to an endpoint of d; assignment is the unique one-to-one matching",
  "transform": "paint the sightline gap with d.color; from d's opposite endpoint, recolor len(gap) path cells with s.color; keep marker pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 20-30 rows x 20-30 cols, square [observed], bg=0
  pair_count: 2 marker-path pairs [observed]; solver supports any count >=1
  paths:
    count: equal to marker count
    structure: 1-pixel-wide unbranched 4-connected chain, exactly 2 endpoints
    length: 7-16 cells
    turns: 1-3 direction changes along the chain
    color: each path has a unique non-bg color, distinct from all marker colors
  markers:
    count: equal to path count
    structure: single isolated pixel
    color: each marker has a unique non-bg color, distinct from all path colors
  sightline:
    each marker has a clear orthogonal bg-only gap to one endpoint of its matched path
    gap_length: 3-6 cells, strictly <= path length (ratio 0.25-0.62)
    the global marker-to-path assignment via sightlines must be unique
  spacing: all objects (paths + markers) are non-adjacent (min Manhattan distance >= 4) [observed]
  density: non-bg cells ~3-7% of grid [observed]
  all_colors_distinct: every path and marker color is unique across the grid

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size (20-30, square) and 2 distinct path colors + 2 distinct marker colors, all non-bg.
    2. For each pair, build the path as a random walk with 1-3 turns, length 7-16.
       Place paths so they don't touch each other or the grid edge (leave room for gap).
    3. For each path, pick one endpoint as the "visible" end. Choose a cardinal direction
       from that endpoint with enough clear space for a gap of 3-6 cells (gap <= path_len).
    4. Place the marker pixel at gap_length+1 cells from the visible endpoint along that direction.
    5. Verify: each marker sees exactly the intended path endpoint via sightline (no other
       path endpoint in any of the marker's 4 sightlines, OR the unique matching still resolves).
    6. Verify no objects overlap or are adjacent; all sightline gaps contain only bg.
```
